  "dtype": "float",
  "citation": "McCullagh, P. (1980), Regression Models for Ordinal Data, Journal of the Royal Statistical Society Series B 42(2), 109-142, DOI 10.1111/j.2517-6161.1980.tb01109.x. Table 1, p. 111, `Tonsil size of carriers and non-carriers of Streptococcus pyogenes`, reads carriers 19, 29 and 24 over the three ordered sizes against non-carriers 497, 560 and 269, totalling 72, 1326 and 1398. The original data are Holmes, M. C. and Williams, R. E. O. (1954), which the paper cites for them.",
  "notes": "THE COLUMN MARGIN OF THE SAME PUBLISHED TABLE, one row per child, in the same order as its companion mccullagh_1980_tonsil_size: within each size the carriers come first and the non-carriers follow. It sums to 72 x 0.5 - 1326 x 0.5 = -627, and 72 of its 1398 rows are carriers, which is the column total the page prints.\n\nCODED +1/2 AND -1/2 RATHER THAN 1 AND 0, AND THAT IS THE PAPER'S OWN PARAMETERISATION rather than a convenience. McCullagh's equation (2.4) writes the two groups as `theta_j -/+ Delta/2`, so the group enters symmetrically and the slope IS his Delta; coding it 1 and 0 would shift both cut points by half of it and the theta_1 = -0.810 and theta_2 = 1.061 the page prints would no longer be what an ordered model reports.\n\nWHAT IT DOES NOT SUPPORT. Carriage of Streptococcus pyogenes is the only covariate the page publishes a fit for, and the paper is explicit that the direction of the relationship is an assumption of the analysis rather than a finding of it."
